    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\nRedux architecture should be judged by state ownership, update frequency, consistency, persistence, server synchronization, performance, and operational complexity.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nIdentify the ownership boundary, trace one small state/action/input flow, then implement the smallest correct version.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ntype State = { value: number };\n\nfunction reducer(\n  state: State,\n  action: { type: \"increment\" },\n): State {\n  return action.type === \"increment\"\n    ? { value: state.value + 1 }\n    : state;\n}\n\nconsole.log(\n  reducer({ value: 0 }, { type: \"increment\" }),\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / PRACTICAL React/Redux TypeScript:\n```tsx\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\n  name: \"counter\",\n  initialState: { value: 0 },\n  reducers: {\n    increment: state => {\n      state.value += 1;\n    },\n  },\n});\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\n{ value: 1 }\n```\n\nStep 6 — Complexity / trade-off.\nUse Redux for state whose scope/coordination warrants it, not as a universal default.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases.\nCheck empty/invalid input, stale async work, cleanup, duplicate updates, accessibility, serialization, and large-data performance where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway.\nExplain the mechanism, ownership boundary, failure mode, testing strategy, performance implications, and why the practical approach is preferred.",
